WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.580
- But let's get to my first guest.

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:04.805
I am very excited slash really nervous.

00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:07.370
She is a true Hollywood legend,

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.990
a children's book author and
just absolutely all class.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.410
She is a true lady.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.770
Dame Julie Andrews, are you there?

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.410
- I am here

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.260
and you're there.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.832
I can see that you are.

00:00:18.832 --> 00:00:19.970
- I am.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.480
I'm very nervous and excited.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.910
I have one quick question

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.050
'cause I actually don't know this answer.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.370
Do I say 'Dame' every time or -?

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.440
- No, certainly, no. (laughs)

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.520
- [Kelly] Oh, I don't know.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.580
I don't know.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.700
- Julie or Jules, one of the two

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.060
- Okay, I'll call you...

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.910
Dame is just a lovely, personal thing

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.690
and it's just something that one keeps

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.820
kind of quietly to one's self.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.820
Some people use it.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.530
I don't but I'm very honored to be so.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.120
- Well, I know every time
if I've ever had to say

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.040
'Sir Elton John' I
always have to say 'Sir'.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.373
So I'm like 'I'm going to get this right.'

00:00:58.373 --> 00:00:59.960
(laughs)

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:00.950
- So how are you?

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.170
Because, I mean, this
whole pandemic's happening.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.003
Are you safe?

00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:04.890
Are you healthy?

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:05.747
- I am safe

00:01:05.747 --> 00:01:06.930
and I am healthy.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.805
I am, funnily enough, and I'm
sure you find the same thing

00:01:10.805 --> 00:01:13.990
I feel busier than ever.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:17.930
Because one is doing so
much via the internet

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.950
and using the computer so much

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.870
and contacting people

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.470
and being in touch with families

00:01:24.344 --> 00:01:25.470
and doing Zoom calls

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.630
and God knows what else.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:28.463
- Yeah, no.

00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:29.690
My husband and I were
just talking about that.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.210
I'm like, 'I've never felt busier'

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.436
and I already was really busy.

00:01:33.436 --> 00:01:35.810
Because now it's all the jobs

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.210
plus taking care of the children
and cooking and cleaning.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.993
All the stuff that I don't
normally do all of it.

00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:45.040
- (laughs) How long have you
been married then, Kelly?

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.040
- We've been together for about 8 years.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.360
We've been married for, like, 7.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.536
Yeah.

00:01:49.536 --> 00:01:50.369
- Aw

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:51.202
- He's good.

00:01:51.202 --> 00:01:54.210
You know, there have been
points in this pandemic,

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.678
where I'm like, 'you need to go'.

00:01:56.678 --> 00:01:57.730
(laughs)

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.250
And he feels the same.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.840
So it's a lot of togetherness time with 3,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.360
we have 4 children but 3 are
in our house with us right now.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.436
- Oh, my.

00:02:05.436 --> 00:02:06.389
- Because our oldest one is 18.

00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:08.410
But enough about me,
no one cares about me.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:09.243
They care about you.

00:02:09.243 --> 00:02:10.076
- No, we do.

00:02:10.076 --> 00:02:11.476
I'm interested.

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:12.610
- You shouldn't be.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:13.510
You're way cooler.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.840
Um, so my point though is, I
know you won't remember this

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.070
because it is like a non-event for you

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.775
but just so you know,

00:02:20.775 --> 00:02:23.440
obviously the Princess Diaries you were in

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.690
and I was the single from the movie.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:27.523
The song was called 'Breakaway'

00:02:27.523 --> 00:02:29.894
and so they invited me
to that premier years ago

00:02:29.894 --> 00:02:31.610
and you don't know this

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.120
but I never say 'Hi' to people generally

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.450
'cause I get too nervous

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.870
but our clothes, my dress,

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.420
it was a green dress with flowers

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.170
and it grazed your clothes as
we passed on the red carpet.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.270
- I'm so glad.

00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:46.260
- I'm going to tell you what,

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.410
you think it's so silly but
it made my entire, like,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.580
I've talked about it for a year.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.410
I was like, 'I was legit
inches from Julie Andrews.'

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.940
- But Kelly, didn't you
send me something once,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.770
you sent me a recording
of yours or something.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:02:59.603
- I did.

00:02:59.603 --> 00:03:01.340
It was because I had a
Christmas record coming out

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.120
and I was hanging out with Trisha Yearwood

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.060
and her publicist is,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.078
do you have the same
publicist or something?

00:03:07.078 --> 00:03:09.510
- I think it's the same agent.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.343
I'm not sure.

00:03:10.343 --> 00:03:11.176
- Agent, that's what it was.

00:03:11.176 --> 00:03:12.009
It was somebody.

00:03:12.009 --> 00:03:12.930
She was at the house and she was like,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.843
'Oh, yeah, I work with Julie Andrews.'

00:03:14.843 --> 00:03:15.965
I was like, 'Wait, what?'

00:03:15.965 --> 00:03:17.580
(Julie laughs)

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.413
And I was like,

00:03:18.413 --> 00:03:20.214
'Oh my god, I just covered
My Favorite Things.'

00:03:20.214 --> 00:03:21.940
It's completely different.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:23.500
It's a different kind of version.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.570
- Yeah, you sent it to me, I think.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:26.635
Yeah.

00:03:26.635 --> 00:03:28.120
- And she said, she was like,
'Well, I'll send it to her.'

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.398
And I was like,

00:03:29.398 --> 00:03:31.110
'Oh my god, Julie Andrews is
going to have something I did.'

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.293
It was really crazy.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.760
- I love the way you sing, Kelly.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:35.950
It's lovely.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.080
- Oh, thank you so much.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:37.990
Thank you so much.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:38.823
I try.

00:03:38.823 --> 00:03:41.140
Some days I sound, you know, nice.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.030
And some days I sound like Janis Joplin

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.550
'cause I'm tired but whatever. (laughs)

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.042
- We all do, believe me.

00:03:47.042 --> 00:03:50.620
- I was like, 'I just can't
believe this is happening.'

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:51.980
But this pandemic though,

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.970
it's been separating people
but also uniting them

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:57.210
and I heard that you had
said something interesting

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.130
and you said that it
reminded you of World War 2,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:03:59.963
a little bit.

00:03:59.963 --> 00:04:01.140
How so?

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.700
- I was a young child during the war

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.840
and it was that fear of the unknown

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.820
that lack of control that you have.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.560
A little bit like when
you are in an earthquake

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.393
or something.

00:04:12.393 --> 00:04:13.226
There's nothing you can do

00:04:13.226 --> 00:04:14.834
and you don't know when
it's going to happen.

00:04:14.834 --> 00:04:19.834
But the war made everyone
in England where I was born

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.930
we bonded like crazy

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:26.650
because we absolutely
had to support each other

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.210
and be strong for each other

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:31.610
and try to be brave and calm and so on

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.470
and its very much the same way
now with this huge pandemic

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:39.390
and everybody's terrified
and worried and scared.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:43.826
It's very similar so, for me,
it brings back those memories

00:04:43.826 --> 00:04:44.659
a little bit.

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:47.390
- I did, randomly, I did
this interview in the UK

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.044
a while back while promoting a record

00:04:49.044 --> 00:04:50.570
and Vanessa Redgrave's was there promoting

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.140
a project she was doing

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.805
and she actually talked
about that in the interview.

00:04:54.805 --> 00:04:55.638
- Did she?

00:04:55.638 --> 00:04:56.471
Oh, interesting.

00:04:56.471 --> 00:04:57.304
Well, it was.

00:04:57.304 --> 00:05:00.070
You know, it was huge for
those of us who were...

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.440
Well, if it hadn't been
for you wonderful Americans

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.200
we'd probably not be here today,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.700
but you came in to help rescue

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.840
and do wonderful things for us.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.280
My parents and I, I was
in Wardle in musical

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.210
and we went to an american army base

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.860
and our salary was a good hearty meal

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.120
and I was given a T-bone steak

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.207
which I'd never seen in my life.

00:05:25.207 --> 00:05:27.720
And I'd never seen so
much meat in my life.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.600
That entire T-bone would
have fed our family

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:32.793
during the rationing.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.055
Four of us, five of us, you know.

00:05:36.055 --> 00:05:38.862
It was the most wonderful supper.

00:05:38.862 --> 00:05:43.312
So seeing the way the...

00:05:43.312 --> 00:05:45.250
I guess it was the army,

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.150
I think, I don't think it was air force

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.420
but what ever it was,
it was an amazing visit.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:51.253
It really was.

00:05:51.253 --> 00:05:53.072
And I had to entertain, so.

00:05:53.072 --> 00:05:55.210
- Oh my god, I bet they freaked out.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:56.043
That's so cool.

00:05:56.043 --> 00:05:56.876
That's so cool.

00:05:56.876 --> 00:05:58.680
And you said you were younger, right?

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.550
- Oh, yes, I was very young, yes.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:05.410
But it was, well I started
singing when I was about 7 or 8.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:10.410
So yeah, and the war ended
when I was about 10, I guess.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.363
- Wow.

00:06:11.363 --> 00:06:12.630
So what exactly is the podcast.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:13.570
Tell us about it.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.210
- Well, it's just called 'Julie's Library'

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:21.210
and it's about, I and my
daughter are in my little library

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.840
and we read books,

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.170
or it's really geared
towards very young children

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:32.210
but hopefully their parents
and grandparent even

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.960
who want to sit with them and listen

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.330
and after we've read
the story we discuss it.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.130
We talk about the ideas.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.160
We talk about words

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.500
and how wonderful they are

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:45.740
and we invite the kids
to send things to us

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:49.220
and they come online
and all kinds of things.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:52.580
It's hopefully comforting

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.530
and something to listen to

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.980
and spark the imagination a little bit

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:01.150
so it was silly not to try
to get it ready for release

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:02.970
and that's what we did.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:06.190
- No, from one parent
to another, thank you.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:07.410
But while we're talking about the podcast,

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:09.714
I do want to obviously bring
in Emma, your daughter.

00:07:09.714 --> 00:07:11.460
Let's bring Emma in.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:12.923
Emma, can you hear us?

00:07:12.923 --> 00:07:13.756
Are you here?

00:07:13.756 --> 00:07:14.589
- I can.

00:07:14.589 --> 00:07:15.422
Can you hear me?

00:07:15.422 --> 00:07:16.290
- [Kelly] I can.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.830
- Hi, nice to meet you.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.931
- All right, well we were
just talking about the podcast

00:07:20.931 --> 00:07:23.130
'Julie's Green Room' that's
what you're calling it, right?

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.590
- No, 'Julie's Library'

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:26.840
but 'Julie's Green Room' is something

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.730
that I'd love to talk about as well.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.500
Because, well Emma do you
want to talk about that?

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.610
- So, 'Julie's Green
Room' is a Netflix series

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:35.830
that we did about four years ago together.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.260
And it stars Mom as the director

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:40.840
of her own small theater company

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.226
and she has students who
come and learn theater,

00:07:44.226 --> 00:07:48.770
how to produce a musical
basically from her

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:52.320
and those were puppets created
by the Jim Henson company.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.400
Its a lovely show that celebrates the arts

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.940
and we've been finding and
hearing that people have been

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.543
discovering it again during quarantine.

00:08:00.509 --> 00:08:02.333
- On Netflix, Kelly.

00:08:02.333 --> 00:08:03.166
- On Netflix.

00:08:03.166 --> 00:08:05.410
And Mom has embarked on
a social media campaign

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:06.950
of putting out some little videos

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:09.110
in conjunction with each of the episodes

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:10.780
on her Facebook page.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.030
- And also we have wonderful guest stars

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:14.420
Josh Groben, we have.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.909
- [Kelly] I love him!

00:08:15.909 --> 00:08:16.742
- And Carol Burnett

00:08:16.742 --> 00:08:17.700
and all sorts of wonderful people.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:18.533
- [Emma] Sara Bareilles,

00:08:18.533 --> 00:08:19.640
and Alec Baldwin

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.200
and lots of fun people.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.700
- That's amazing.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:26.540
I mean, y'all also, I know this because

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:29.320
whenever I was actually
writing my own children's book

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.030
I was in there and I went into the store

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:34.200
and I kind of looked at all
those stories and I had no idea

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.350
but y'all had written, what
is it, 30 books together?

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.183
Children's books?

00:08:38.183 --> 00:08:39.270
- It's actually over 30 now,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:40.103
I think it's 32.

00:08:40.103 --> 00:08:41.198
- Now, yeah.

00:08:41.198 --> 00:08:42.720
32, 33.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:43.980
- Oh my gosh, that's a lot of work.

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:45.590
Because honestly, I've written a couple

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:47.150
and they're almost harder,

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:48.500
like the cadence and how they,

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.650
'cause mine rhyme as well,

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.456
so it's just like the cadence of it is...

00:08:51.456 --> 00:08:53.857
- Wow, good for you, Kelly.

00:08:53.857 --> 00:08:55.380
- It's very difficult.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.420
Oh, thank you.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.560
But it was just a really interesting thing

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:00.710
Like, how does that work?

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:01.803
Like, whenever y'all have...

00:09:01.803 --> 00:09:04.890
Even me with my editor we
have creative differences

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.690
like, how does that work between you two?

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.944
Who wins in that creative argument?

00:09:08.944 --> 00:09:10.183
- The best idea wins.

00:09:10.183 --> 00:09:11.420
- The best idea wins.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:12.877
That's our saying.

00:09:12.877 --> 00:09:16.660
- If we stumble on something,

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.960
there are two or three things that work

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.780
but one is for sure,

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:23.730
our mantra, is that the best idea wins.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.720
If one of us is more
passionate about something

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.593
or fervently believes that this
is the way to go and so on,

00:09:30.774 --> 00:09:34.010
the other one seems to recognize that.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.400
And then the other is
to take a little break

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.800
for a cup of tea or even a
bathroom break once in a while

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.020
and it does wonders for the brain

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:44.310
because you suddenly
stop what you are doing

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.010
and while you are fiddling
around with a cup of tea

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:51.410
or something, you get
back on track somehow.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.470
I can't put any better
way to say that, can you?

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.410
- Yeah, the answer comes.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.280
- Yeah, the answer does come.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.650
It's 'cause you've freed
the brain for a moment.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:00.670
- Yeah, I know.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.320
Even writing songs, I'm like that.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:03.720
I have to walk away and come back

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.500
and kind of escape the bubble.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:06.487
- Of course.

00:10:07.343 --> 00:10:09.550
- My question is, though, I
grew up around so many books

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.560
and that's why I'm an avid reader now,

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.780
God bless my mom, I'm glad
that she instilled that in me.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.070
But my favorite book, I can't ever escape

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:20.180
and I'm really getting into it now

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:21.480
with my five year-old little girl

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.672
who now is in love with it as well

00:10:23.672 --> 00:10:24.750
But was 'Matilda'.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:25.583
That was my favorite book.

00:10:25.583 --> 00:10:26.540
- Oh!

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.840
- Yeah, it was my first, like,
big book to read by myself

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.430
and I was wondering
since y'all are authors

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:32.100
and you love reading as well.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.170
What would you suggest?

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:35.160
What were your favorite
books when you were kids?

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.720
What's a story that you
really love that you think

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.721
that parents should maybe
get and read with them?

00:10:38.721 --> 00:10:39.920
- You go first, Mom.
- Emma, you go.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.382
- You go first, darling.

00:10:41.382 --> 00:10:42.899
(all laugh)

00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:44.571
- Oh, you're so nice.

00:10:44.571 --> 00:10:48.160
- We actually finish each
other's sentences so...

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:49.653
That's funny, darling.

00:10:49.653 --> 00:10:51.960
- Oh my god, I hope my
daughter and I are like y'all.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.393
This is adorable.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.980
- Any way, we do finish
each others sentences.

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:57.980
Go ahead, Em.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:59.235
- All right.

00:10:59.235 --> 00:11:01.140
So for me, that book was
'The Phantom Toll Booth'.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.087
- I love that one.

00:11:03.087 --> 00:11:05.810
- That was my rainy day
book, my go-to book,

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:09.340
and I just loved the idea
of being in your own house

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.490
and getting in a toy car

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:11.960
and driving through a
little toy toll booth

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.316
and being in a totally different world.

00:11:14.316 --> 00:11:15.320
(Julie laughs)

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.940
- And I remember what I loved
best was that in that book

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.030
one of the places he
drove to was a town called

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:24.840
'Dictionopolis' where people
literally bought and sold words

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.610
and words had flavors colors to them

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.190
and I was just so
completely enchanted by that

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:31.370
and I think that's part of the reason

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.390
why I love words to this day.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:34.320
I really do.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.660
- And y'all, is that the podcast

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.320
that y'all involve with the words, right?

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.482
Where you ask kids their
favorite words and things?

00:11:39.482 --> 00:11:40.570
- [Julie] Yes.
- [Emma] Yes, exactly.

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:41.839
- It's really cool that y'all
are innovative with that

00:11:41.839 --> 00:11:44.390
and you include them
and your engaging them.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:46.603
That's a really, really
clever thing to do.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:52.140
- From your lips to all the
children that are listening,

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.450
I hope they like it.

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:54.870
- No, my kids, my kids,

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.580
especially my daughter's
learning to read right now

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:58.330
so she loves, like anytime
she gets a new word,

00:11:58.330 --> 00:11:59.747
she feels so proud.

00:11:59.747 --> 00:12:02.400
So what are some of your favorite words?

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:03.330
If you love words so much?

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.080
Emma, what are some of your favorite ones?

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:06.390
- Family.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:07.223
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:12:07.223 --> 00:12:08.056
- Is definitely one.

00:12:08.056 --> 00:12:11.461
- We actually sort of
started to play a game

00:12:11.461 --> 00:12:13.710
recently, believe it or not,

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.590
because this is such a
sad and terrible time

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:18.670
and I was trying to say to the family

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.600
'cause we get on Zoom, all of us, you know

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:22.650
I've got children in California

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:24.580
and I've got relatives
and family in England

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:25.413
and so on

00:12:25.413 --> 00:12:26.520
so I was saying to them all

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.709
think of the nicest word you can think of

00:12:28.709 --> 00:12:33.410
to cheer us all today and
we'll each contribute.

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:35.580
And my word, I think,

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.120
the first one I came up with was 'spring'.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:38.953
Wasn't it, Emma?

00:12:38.953 --> 00:12:40.290
- It was, yeah.

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:44.220
Another favorite of mine is 'hope'

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.130
and it's not just because
it's a beautiful word

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:48.160
for right now but it's
also my daughter's name

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:51.793
and I think that's why I
chose it, or why we chose it.

00:12:52.724 --> 00:12:54.960
- It's Hope and Sam, right?

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:56.120
- That's right, yeah.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:57.280
- Those are great names.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.310
They are great names.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.740
- In fact, Kelly, Sam is sitting
right in front of me today

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:04.820
because he is a phenomenal sound engineer

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:07.920
and he's been helping me get
on to this recording today

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.830
because I know nothing about it.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:11.010
- Yes!

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:12.290
Shout out to Sam!

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:13.355
What up!

00:13:13.355 --> 00:13:14.600
- Aye.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:17.100
Yup, he's sitting over
there with his mask on

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.110
being a very good son.

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:20.270
- I'm going to play the game.

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:21.103
One of my favorite,

00:13:21.103 --> 00:13:23.907
I think possibly my
favorite word is 'magic'.

00:13:23.907 --> 00:13:25.730
- Oh, that's great!

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:27.360
- Yeah, not just 'cause it's escapism.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.500
I like the magic of it
and you know that word.

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:33.220
But I like that anything can be magical.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:36.360
Like, a moment, it
could be snowing outside

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:38.080
or it could be something
your kid says to you

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.120
that kind of breaks your
heart in the best way.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:40.953
- I get it.

00:13:40.953 --> 00:13:42.150
Absolutely.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:43.300
- Yeah.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.010
- Well, honestly, there are so many

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:46.899
if you start thinking about them

00:13:46.899 --> 00:13:47.732
'Joy.'

00:13:49.079 --> 00:13:50.080
- 'Wonder.'

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:51.450
'Wonder' is another favorite of ours.

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:55.230
- Yeah, we have a motto
for our publishing company

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.540
which is words, wisdom, wonder.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:01.140
Words lead to wisdom which lead to wonder.

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:03.840
And we call them our three W's.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:04.750
- I love that.

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:06.280
I love y'all as a team.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:07.617
You're so great.

00:14:07.617 --> 00:14:08.450
(all laugh)

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.040
- It's a great, great pleasure.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:13.540
I mean, we didn't know when
we began writing together

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:16.300
that it would be such a joy

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:21.090
but can you imagine, Kelly,
if your little girl at five,

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.030
grew up and became an equal,

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:27.100
a beloved equal and you
were writing with her.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.770
It's quite a wonderful gift.

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:31.820
And that's the way it was for us.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:35.360
We did fight a little bit
when you were young, Emma.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:36.740
- We did.

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:39.540
- I think that connection,
nothing beats it.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.380
When you have something
that your kid likes,

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.510
even, like, my kid, no one in my family,

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:45.760
this is going to sound ridiculous,

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.550
no one in my family like beets,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:48.840
But I love beets.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.350
And so my daughter came along
and, like, she's the one.

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:52.750
She's, like, obsessed with beets with me.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.570
So we have beets and
blue cheese all the time.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:56.870
- Well, as we say in my family,

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.760
you share the beets gene, right?

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.484
- Yeah, you either hate them or love them.

00:15:02.484 --> 00:15:03.534
- That's right, yeah.

